WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:02.210
- Rube Foster starts

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.449
the Negro National League in 1920.

00:00:04.449 --> 00:00:05.440
- When the league started,

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.633
there were eight teams.

00:00:07.601 --> 00:00:09.080
- The beauty of starting

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.350
a baseball league during that period,

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:14.810
gave an opportunity for men
of color to play baseball,

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:18.980
where there was no opportunity
in the white major leagues.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.780
The challenges that Andrew "Rube" Foster

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:23.960
had in 1920 when he started
the Negro National League

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:27.820
was economic stability,
a solid league schedule

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.170
and the availability of ballparks.

00:00:30.170 --> 00:00:31.610
- When we look at the Negro leagues

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:33.400
in comparison to the major leagues,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.140
talent? No difference.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.750
But the major leagues had more money,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.870
and they had their own stadiums.

00:00:39.870 --> 00:00:42.410
That's really the one
fundamental difference

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:43.870
between the two leagues.

00:00:43.870 --> 00:00:46.130
- Black owners, for the most part,

00:00:46.130 --> 00:00:47.830
they didn't own their own stadiums.

00:00:47.830 --> 00:00:50.160
They had to rent stadiums
from white teams.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.740
That cut into profits.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:53.530
So they spent a lot of time on the road.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:56.265
They were barnstorming constantly.

00:00:56.265 --> 00:00:57.760
- You have to have your own bus.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:02.290
You can't ride the train
during the era of segregated.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.507
- They can't stay in hotels.

00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:05.080
- Quite a few of them spent their time

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.140
sleeping on their bus.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.840
- They can't eat at restaurants.

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:10.854
- Major league ballplayers

00:01:10.854 --> 00:01:14.650
made between six and
seven times the amount

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.120
that the average Negro
league ballplayer made.

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:20.840
- Racism is real in the United States.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:22.910
And so it's not different
for baseball players,

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:25.240
even if they are great at what they do.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.730
We can see gaps in coverage,

00:01:26.730 --> 00:01:28.860
and a lot of that is because the racism

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.430
that they endured from the white press

00:01:31.430 --> 00:01:33.300
that didn't feel that what they were doing

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.363
on segregated ball fields was valuable.

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.470
- When you say "Negro leagues,"

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:41.760
it actually encompassed many leagues.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.130
You had a Southern League.

00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.220
You had the Eastern Colored League.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.760
You had the Negro American League.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.970
- The competing leagues
caused significant problems

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:52.780
for each other, because quite honestly,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.230
it came down to market share.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:58.300
Rube Foster decided he
wanted to expand to the East.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:00.950
Well, that was gonna infringe upon

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.330
the other major Black clubs in the East.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:05.961
So they formed their own league.

00:02:05.961 --> 00:02:07.140
- In 1924,

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.910
the first Colored World Series.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.870
- The Kansas City Monarchs and Hilldale,

00:02:10.870 --> 00:02:12.730
out of the Eastern Colored League,

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.710
they decided they were
gonna play each other.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:17.920
- Kansas City Monarchs won that series

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.940
against Ed Bolden's Hilldale Club.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:25.053
It started a new wave of
popularity and acceptance.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:27.660
- [Reporter:] The tremendous crowds,

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.019
which you see gathered outside,

00:02:29.019 --> 00:02:30.900
are due to the greatest crash

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:31.733
in the history
[Car horn honking]

00:02:31.733 --> 00:02:33.333
of the New York Stock Exchange.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.190
- When we have an economic setback

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.680
like the Great Depression,
white America catches a cold.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:41.517
Black America catches pneumonia.

00:02:41.517 --> 00:02:42.510
- A lot of Black people

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.050
were out of work. They just
couldn't support the game,

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:48.130
and that was the majority
of their fanbase.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:51.560
- Rube Foster was on
league business in 1925

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.620
in Indianapolis when he
was exposed to a gas leak

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.150
inside of his hotel room.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:00.710
Never quite the same; likely
suffered brain damage.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:04.690
As a result, was subject
to fits of violent outrage,

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:07.880
forcing his family to
institutionalize him,

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:11.080
where three years later,
he dies of a heart attack.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.970
Negro leagues had lost
its spiritual leader.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:16.720
- In 1931, the Negro
National League folded.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.530
- The death of Rube Foster

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.210
devastated the Negro National League.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:23.670
But then you couple that
with the Great Depression,

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.773
and it had virtually no
shot to continue to operate.

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.230
- Even though the Negro
National League ended,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.090
there's always somebody else to be able

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:33.240
to pick up that mantle.

00:03:34.244 --> 00:03:35.077
- Gus Greenlee

00:03:35.077 --> 00:03:38.070
was the Rube Foster of the 1930s.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:42.110
- Gus Greenlee would
revive the Negro Leagues.

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.280
- They called him Big Red.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:44.830
Had two brothers became a doctor,

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:46.940
but he chose the street life.

00:03:46.940 --> 00:03:49.840
- Gus Greenlee was a numbers runner.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.780
He, before that, was a rum runner.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.563
- He was also an owner of a nightclub.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.560
When the only bank in Pittsburgh fell

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:00.210
because of the Depression,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:04.360
Gus Greenlee became the
banker of the Black community.

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.820
This man was brilliant.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.150
- He got into baseball when some members

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:09.720
of the Pittsburgh Crawfords team

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.230
came into his club that he owned in 1930

00:04:12.230 --> 00:04:14.530
and asked him to invest in their team.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.070
They were a semi-pro
sandlot team at the time

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:19.320
and, you know, were facing
financial challenges

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:21.840
like so many independent Black clubs did.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.950
So he purchases the
team, puts them on salary

00:04:24.950 --> 00:04:27.130
and then kinda sets out to become

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:30.260
the king of Black baseball
in the Pittsburgh area.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.300
He starts the second Negro
National League in late 1932.

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:36.500
They play their first season in 1933.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:38.290
- And really at that point,

00:04:38.290 --> 00:04:40.760
take the Negro leagues to its apex,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:43.960
creating the vaunted
East-West All-Star Game,

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.533
debuting the same year

00:04:45.533 --> 00:04:48.740
as Major League Baseball's All-Star Game.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:51.130
- We see in the 1940s
that they were drawing

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:53.850
more fans at this Black All-Star Game

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.910
than Major League Baseball was.

00:04:55.910 --> 00:04:59.173
This was the biggest sporting
event in Black America.

00:05:00.290 --> 00:05:05.290
- December 7, 1941, a date
which will live in infamy.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:12.890
- World War II adversely
impacted both leagues,

00:05:12.890 --> 00:05:15.860
but it didn't hurt the
Negro Leagues quite as bad

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:17.540
as it hurt Major League Baseball.

00:05:17.540 --> 00:05:19.768
The Negro leagues certainly
had its fair share

00:05:19.768 --> 00:05:23.270
of athletes who served,
but the superstar players

00:05:23.270 --> 00:05:25.500
from the Negro leagues were too old,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:28.930
where that young major league
stars got called into service,

00:05:28.930 --> 00:05:31.320
Major League Baseball
literally had to shut down.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:33.810
But the Negro leagues were still going,

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:38.810
and white fans came over to
watch Negro leagues baseball.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.000
- Defense hiring means
that a lot of Black people

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.960
who'd been out of work for
so long get well-paying jobs.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.320
Now they have extra money,
not just to make ends meet,

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:49.260
but to actually come to games.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:50.890
Black teams have more money

00:05:50.890 --> 00:05:53.240
because more fans are coming to games.

00:05:53.240 --> 00:05:55.850
- It was the greatest
experience of the week.

00:05:55.850 --> 00:05:57.750
- Everybody's dressed up.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:06:00.440
- Women would bring fried
chicken, pass a basket

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.660
of home-baked bread around.
An awesome experience,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.870
and we hadn't even got
to the ballgame yet.

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:09.843
- We're talking about some of
the greatest baseball players

00:06:09.843 --> 00:06:12.970
to ever put on a baseball uniform.

00:06:12.970 --> 00:06:16.700
- You can drop names all day
long, like Roy Campanella.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:19.510
- Cool Papa Bell the fastest guy to play.

00:06:19.510 --> 00:06:20.670
- Monte Irvin.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:22.680
- Then you add Oscar Charleston.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:24.330
- Bullet Rogan gonna hit a home run

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.320
and strike out 15 or 16.

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:27.300
- Buck Leonard.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:28.600
- Piper Davis.

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.580
- Guess who's the greatest home run hitter

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.185
that ever played the game?

00:06:32.185 --> 00:06:33.063
Josh Gibson.

00:06:33.063 --> 00:06:36.358
- Everybody wanted to see Josh Gibson.

00:06:36.358 --> 00:06:39.010
The legendary Satchel Paige.

00:06:39.010 --> 00:06:40.000
- Satchel Paige

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.047
could do a doubleheader by himself.

00:06:42.047 --> 00:06:45.710
- Satchel was so big, when
the Monarchs went out to play,

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:47.974
if Satchel didn't pitch,
the people felt like,

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:50.000
"We want our money back."

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:53.200
- When Black servicemen came
back home to this country,

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.063
they still were subjected
to a lot of the things

00:06:56.063 --> 00:06:59.190
that were going on in this
country before they left.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.760
- America had to wake up to the fact

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:04.160
that we were a segregated
and racist country,

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.163
and maybe it was time to
integrate our national pastime.

